  "title": "CodeGrep: An RL-Trained Retrieval Agent for LLM Coding Agents",
  "event_hint": "CodeGrep: 14B retrieval agent trained with GRPO to reduce coding agent file exploration overhead",
  "problem": "Modern LLM coding agents (Claude Code, OpenHands) spend most of their token budget finding the file to patch rather than patching it. On SWE-Bench Verified, a 30B OpenHands agent averages 23 rounds and 631K tokens per resolved issue, with a large fraction consumed by grep, glob, and view_file calls during ambiguous exploration loops—often 20+ rounds in wrong directions before the agent gives up.",
  "mechanism": "CodeGrep is a 14B-parameter retrieval agent built on Qwen3-14B-Instruct, trained end-to-end with GRPO to issue multi-turn parallel grep/glob/read tool calls and return candidate files to a frozen downstream coding agent. It exposes three read-only primitives (regex search, path matching, file contents) mirroring developer navigation. At each turn it emits up to 8 tool calls executed concurrently. During inference, CodeGrep takes the issue description, emits candidate files, and those files are injected into the prompt of a frozen OpenHands downstream agent—only the retriever is trained.",
  "evidence": [
    {
      "claim": "CodeGrep resolve rate on SWE-Bench Verified",
      "value": 27.0,
      "baseline": "no-retrieval baseline: 25.8%",
      "condition": "500 SWE-Bench Verified instances, OpenHands with Qwen3-30B-A3B-Instruct-2507, temperature 0",
      "source_locator": "PDF Page 7, Table 3 / Section 4.3 Downstream evaluation"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Rounds reduction on resolved SWE-Bench instances",
      "value": 19.6,
      "baseline": "no-retrieval baseline: 23.0 rounds",
      "condition": "resolved SWE-Bench Verified instances, CodeGrep v3 injection",
      "source_locator": "PDF Page 7, Table 3 / Section 4.3 Downstream evaluation"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Tokens reduction on resolved SWE-Bench instances",
      "value": 514000,
      "baseline": "no-retrieval baseline: 631K tokens",
      "condition": "resolved SWE-Bench Verified instances, CodeGrep v3 injection",
      "source_locator": "PDF Page 7, Table 3 / Section 4.3 Downstream evaluation"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Precision threshold for retrieval utility",
      "value": 0.677,
      "baseline": "BM25: 0.375 (degrades), Jina: 0.445 (neutral)",
      "condition": "file-level precision on internal evaluation set, downstream utility regime classification",
      "source_locator": "PDF Page 8, Table 2 / Section 5.1 precision threshold analysis"
    },
    {
      "claim": "CodeGrep tool-use efficiency",
      "value": 2.3,
      "baseline": "BM25: N/A, CodeGrep v1: 3.8 turns",
      "condition": "average tool-use turns per rollout on internal evaluation set, CodeGrep v3",
      "source_locator": "PDF Page 7, Table 2 / Section 4.2 Retriever evaluation"
    }
  ],
  "evaluation_context": "Evaluation on SWE-Bench Verified (500 instances) using OpenHands with Qwen3-30B-A3B-Instruct-2507 as downstream agent, temperature 0, 100-round max. Baseline without retrieval resolves 25.8% at 23.0 rounds and 631K tokens per resolved issue. Training uses 67K open-source agent trajectories mined via CATM, with GLM-5.1-FP8 as judge model. Training on single 8xB200 node with LoRA, 27-hour wall-clock per run, early-stop at step 897 of 2000-step budget. Environment supports thousands of unique (repo,commit) pairs with 64 asyncio semaphore for tool concurrency, max 8 parallel tools per turn, 32 max tool execs per rollout.",
  "limitations": "Evaluation limited to SWE-Bench Verified benchmark only—no production deployment data or real-world coding workflows reported. The line-range component was removed because downstream OpenHands editor has no view_range argument, making predicted line ranges dead information at inference time. Training data includes CATM-only entries with no gold line ranges by construction, introducing systematic label bias. The downstream agent is frozen (OpenHands), so results may not generalize to other coding agents like Claude Code. Precision threshold regime characterization based on only three retrievers (BM25, Jina, CodeGrep). End-to-end wall-clock latency not reported, only token and round metrics.",
  "project_relevance": "Directly relevant to Agent semantic acceleration focus on reducing repetitive Read, Grep, Glob, and code exploration. CodeGrep demonstrates a clear precision threshold (≈0.45-0.50) below which retrieval degrades downstream agent and above which it buys efficiency. This directly addresses current questions about whether repository indices truly reduce end-to-end task latency—CodeGrep shows 15% rounds reduction and 19% tokens reduction while preserving resolve rate. The work quantifies tool call overhead (2.3 average turns per rollout) and shows retrieval must cross precision ≈0.677 to translate into downstream efficiency. However, CodeGrep does not address repository index construction cost, false positive rates, error recovery overhead, or whether parallel tool calls introduce correctness risk—limitations that project context specifically asks for.",
  "primary_source_resolved": true,
  "quality_score": 68,
  "source_notes": [
    "Document fetched from arXiv with fetch_status=FETCHED, raw_fulltext_cache_hit=true.",
    "Evidence pack spans 17,910 characters using balanced-evidence-v2 strategy, covering abstract, introduction, method, evaluation, and appendix sections.",
    "Key metrics from SWE-Bench Verified (500 instances): 27.0% vs 25.8% resolve rate, 15% fewer rounds, 19% fewer tokens.",
    "Precision threshold analysis shows BM25 (0.375) degrades, Jina (0.445) neutral, CodeGrep (0.677) effective."
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    {
      "question": "What is the token cost breakdown per tool type (grep vs glob vs read) for both CodeGrep and the baseline agent?",
      "terms": ["token cost", "grep", "glob", "read", "tool call", "breakdown", "per-tool"]
    },
    {
      "question": "What is the overhead comparison between retrieval injection and direct agent exploration in terms of end-to-end wall-clock latency?",
      "terms": ["wall-clock", "latency", "overhead", "end-to-end", "retrieval injection", "direct exploration"]
    },
    {
      "question": "What are the repository index construction cost, false positive rate, and error recovery overhead for CodeGrep?",
      "terms": ["index construction", "false positive", "error recovery", "overhead", "repository", "CATM"]
    }
  ]
}

